From: FFN <chris.spivey@itl.us>
Subject: [PATCH] MIPS: OCTEON: ffn_reserve=, ffn_mem=auto, and find the FDT by name

Three related changes to arch/mips/cavium-octeon/setup.c, all removing a
per-board constant that this platform had no business needing.

1. ffn_reserve=<base>,<size>[;<base>,<size>...]  (repeatable)

FFN's CP<->MP transports live at fixed low physical addresses. Measured on a
PA-5220 CN73XX at mem=8G: /proc/iomem shows one range, 20300000-7fefffff :
System RAM, covering all of them, and /proc/kpageflags at each base reads 0x400
(KPF_BUDDY) -- identical to an arbitrary free page, and different from kernel
code, which reads 0x0. The pages are in the buddy allocator's free lists while
ffn_cpdpd and ffn_pcnetd mmap them from /dev/mem and write them. This kernel has
CONFIG_DEVMEM without CONFIG_STRICT_DEVMEM, so the mmap succeeds silently.

They are not cvmx_bootmem named blocks and never were: the bases are bare
physical addresses the MP picks, so plat_mem_setup() hands them to
add_memory_region() like any other DRAM. This keeps them out of the memory map
entirely, reusing the mechanism the 256MB PCIe-hole exclusion already uses.
/dev/mem access is unaffected -- without CONFIG_STRICT_DEVMEM, mmap_mem() does
not require the page to be managed RAM -- so the daemons keep working.

The parameter is REPEATABLE, and that is the form to prefer. ';' is u-boot's
command separator and this boot line is executed by u-boot: an unescaped ';'
truncates the line and silently drops every argument after it, which on this
platform means the later ranges plus mem= and the pktbuf=/wqe= FPA sizing whose
absence kills the packet engine. u-boot runs the first half, boots, and reports
nothing. Measured on a PA-5220 DP.

Every diagnostic exists because the failure mode is a region that is quietly
unprotected while the boot line still reads correctly: ranges are page-aligned
outwards, sorted and coalesced (two overlapping ranges would otherwise each
claim the shared bytes and the second would report a short hit, making the
sanity check cry wolf about a region that WAS removed); a malformed entry costs
only itself; rejected entries are counted and reported once at error level,
because a rejected entry never reaches the table and so cannot be caught by the
per-range hit check; and any range that does not fully land is named.

2. ffn_mem=auto[,<reserve>]

plat_mem_setup() already computes the right number and throws it away.
cvmx_bootmem_available_mem(mem_alloc_size) sums the cvmx free blocks at least
one allocation unit large -- precisely what the allocation loop can consume,
since smaller blocks cannot satisfy a request and are skipped, which is why the
memory map has the holes it has. It is then clamped to max_memory, whose
compile-time default is 512 MB, so an 8 GiB CP and a 32 GiB DP both come up with
~442 MB unless someone passes a per-board mem= constant.

ffn_mem=auto is one token that is correct on every board. It does not take
everything: what Linux claims comes out of the cvmx free list, and cvmx needs
that list afterwards for the FPA packet buffers and WQE pools. Starving them
gives "cvmx_fpa3_pool_populate: out of memory" and a dead packet engine, which
presents exactly like a misprogrammed ring and is not one. So a reserve is held
back, derived from the pool sizing already declared on the same command line
(pktbuf=<count>,<size>, wqe=<count>,<size>), doubled for allocator overhead and
the PKO tables, plus a 256 MB margin. A board running no packet engine declares
small pools and gets a small reserve; a 40-core DP declares large ones and gets
a large one.

The derived reserve is a defensible starting point, not a measured total --
cvmx's full later footprint is not enumerable from here -- so the boot log states
the figure and how to raise it. An explicit mem= always wins, detected by
checking whether max_memory still holds its compile-time default, because
prom_init() consumes mem= and does not re-append it to arcs_cmdline.

3. The device tree is found by name

ffn_fdt= made the operator supply the tree's address as a constant. On a PA-5220
CP that constant is 0x80000 -- which is the base of the cvmx_bootmem named block
"__fdt" that u-boot allocated when it built the tree for this board. Read live
from the descriptor at /proc/octeon_info:phy_mem_desc_addr (0x6c108), the
named-block array holds:

    base=0x00080000  size=0x00020000  name=__fdt

and the FDT there checks out: magic d00dfeed, totalsize 0x136f (4975 bytes),
inside the 128 KiB reservation. So look it up by name, which is what the vendor
CP software does for every region it needs -- brdagent modules reach everything
through cvmx_phys_to_ptr on named blocks, never through hardcoded addresses.

Three checks, because a wrong tree is worse than no tree (the bug this path
exists for was fdt_check_header() faulting on an uninitialised fdt_addr and
hanging the boot with no message): the base must be inside DRAM;
fdt_check_header() must pass; and fdt_totalsize() must fit the block, since a
valid header does not mean the reservation is large enough for the tree it
claims. ffn_fdt= is retained and still wins.

Deployment note: the boot line must keep passing ffn_fdt= until this kernel is
the one running. Dropping it against a kernel without this change falls through
to octeon_bootinfo->fdt_addr, which on the PCIe boot path is the uninitialised
0x...756e6b6e6f ("unknown") value, and the boot dies in octeon_irq_init_ciu on
the built-in legacy-CIU tree.

Verified: 38/38 host unit tests against the code extracted verbatim from this
file -- exclusion arithmetic over the real 4 MB chunk pattern (no emitted region
overlaps a reservation; emitted + reserved == fed; every range fully hit), a bad
entry not taking its siblings down, a stale hardcoded size caught as
under-covering its payload, the repeated-parameter form, and the ffn_mem=auto
reserve derivation for both the CP and DP command lines. Cross build is
warning-clean and vmlinux links.
--- a/arch/mips/cavium-octeon/setup.c
+++ b/arch/mips/cavium-octeon/setup.c
@@ -77,6 +77,36 @@
 static unsigned long long reserve_low_mem;
 
 /*
+ * FFN: physical ranges the MP owns, which Linux must not manage.
+ *
+ * FFN's CP<->MP transports -- the cpdp and pcnet rings, the ffn_rootfs
+ * staging area, ffn_mem -- live at fixed physical addresses. The MP writes them
+ * through the PCIe BAR window before Linux starts, and ffn_cpdpd / ffn_pcnetd
+ * then mmap them out of /dev/mem and keep writing. Those bases were picked when
+ * max_memory still capped Linux at 512 MB, so they sat above everything the
+ * kernel managed. With mem=8G they no longer do: they land in ZONE_DMA32 and go
+ * into the buddy allocator, so the kernel hands the very same pages to somebody
+ * else while the daemons are writing them. Nothing catches it -- this kernel has
+ * CONFIG_DEVMEM without CONFIG_STRICT_DEVMEM, so the mmap succeeds silently.
+ *
+ *     ffn_reserve=<base>,<size>[;<base>,<size>...]
+ *
+ * keeps those ranges out of the memory map entirely. That is exactly what the
+ * PCIe-hole exclusion in plat_mem_setup() already does, and for the same
+ * reason, so this reuses that mechanism rather than inventing another.
+ */
+#define FFN_RESERVE_MAX 8
+
+struct ffn_reserve_range {
+	u64 base;
+	u64 size;
+	u64 hit;	/* bytes actually excluded, for the sanity check below */
+};
+
+static struct ffn_reserve_range ffn_reserve[FFN_RESERVE_MAX] __initdata;
+static int ffn_reserve_count __initdata;
+
+/*
  * modified in hernel-entry-init.h, must have an initial value to keep
  * it from being clobbered when bss is zeroed.
  */
@@ -1114,6 +1144,326 @@
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Advance past the current entry to the start of the next one, so that one
+ * malformed entry costs only itself.
+ *
+ * This exists because the obvious alternative -- stop parsing at the first bad
+ * entry -- fails in the worst possible direction. A typo in the middle of the
+ * list would keep the ranges before it, silently drop every range after it, and
+ * leave a boot line that still reads exactly as the operator intended. The
+ * regions that lost their reservation then get corrupted later with nothing
+ * pointing back to the cause. Entries are independent, so they fail
+ * independently.
+ */
+static __init const char *ffn_reserve_next_entry(const char *p)
+{
+	while (*p && *p != ';' && *p != ' ')
+		p++;
+	if (*p == ';')
+		p++;
+	return p;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Parse ffn_reserve=<base>,<size>[;<base>,<size>...].
+ *
+ * The parameter MAY BE REPEATED, and on OCTEON that is the form to prefer:
+ *
+ *     ffn_reserve=0x28000000,1M ffn_reserve=0x29000000,4M
+ *
+ * is equivalent to the ';'-separated list but cannot be damaged in transit.
+ * ';' is u-boot's command separator, and the boot line here is executed by
+ * u-boot: an unescaped ';' truncates the line at that point and every argument
+ * after it is silently lost -- the later ranges, and anything following, which
+ * on this platform includes mem= and the pktbuf=/wqe= FPA sizing whose absence
+ * kills the packet engine. u-boot runs the first half, boots, and reports
+ * nothing. Measured on a PA-5220 DP. A backslash-escaped '\;' survives and the
+ * kernel still sees a plain ';', but relying on every caller to escape
+ * correctly is a worse design than accepting a form that needs no escaping, so
+ * both are supported and repetition is documented as the safe one.
+ *
+ * Bases and sizes both go through memparse(), so 0x28000000,1M works as well as
+ * 0x28000000,0x100000. Ranges are aligned outwards, so a size that is not a
+ * whole number of pages still ends up fully protected, and sorted ascending,
+ * which memory_exclude_range() relies on.
+ */
+static __init void ffn_reserve_parse_one(const char *p, int *rejected)
+{
+	while (*p && *p != ' ') {
+		u64 base, size, end_addr;
+		char *endp;
+
+		if (ffn_reserve_count >= FFN_RESERVE_MAX) {
+			pr_warn("FFN: ffn_reserve: more than %d ranges, ignoring \"%s\"\n",
+				FFN_RESERVE_MAX, p);
+			break;
+		}
+
+		base = memparse(p, &endp);
+		if (endp == p || *endp != ',') {
+			pr_warn("FFN: ffn_reserve: expected <base>,<size> at \"%s\", skipping this entry\n",
+				p);
+			(*rejected)++;
+			p = ffn_reserve_next_entry(p);
+			continue;
+		}
+		p = endp + 1;
+
+		size = memparse(p, &endp);
+		if (endp == p || !size) {
+			pr_warn("FFN: ffn_reserve: bad or zero size at \"%s\", skipping this entry\n",
+				p);
+			(*rejected)++;
+			p = ffn_reserve_next_entry(p);
+			continue;
+		}
+		p = endp;
+
+		/* align outwards: a partial page must still be fully removed */
+		end_addr = PAGE_ALIGN(base + size);
+		base &= PAGE_MASK;
+
+		ffn_reserve[ffn_reserve_count].base = base;
+		ffn_reserve[ffn_reserve_count].size = end_addr - base;
+		ffn_reserve[ffn_reserve_count].hit = 0;
+		ffn_reserve_count++;
+
+		if (*p != ';')
+			break;
+		p++;
+	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * Parse every ffn_reserve= occurrence on the command line, then normalise the
+ * table: sort, coalesce, report.
+ */
+static __init void ffn_reserve_parse(void)
+{
+	const char *occ = arcs_cmdline;
+	int rejected = 0;
+	int i, j;
+
+	while ((occ = strstr(occ, "ffn_reserve=")) != NULL) {
+		occ += sizeof("ffn_reserve=") - 1;
+		if (ffn_reserve_count >= FFN_RESERVE_MAX)
+			break;
+		ffn_reserve_parse_one(occ, &rejected);
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Insertion sort, ascending by base. memory_exclude_range() only ever
+	 * moves the chunk cursor forward, so a lower range applied after a
+	 * higher one would be silently skipped.
+	 */
+	for (i = 1; i < ffn_reserve_count; i++) {
+		struct ffn_reserve_range t = ffn_reserve[i];
+
+		for (j = i - 1; j >= 0 && ffn_reserve[j].base > t.base; j--)
+			ffn_reserve[j + 1] = ffn_reserve[j];
+		ffn_reserve[j + 1] = t;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Coalesce overlapping or touching ranges. Without this, two ranges that
+	 * overlap would each claim the shared bytes, so the second would record
+	 * a short hit and the sanity check at the end of plat_mem_setup() would
+	 * report it as only partly excluded -- when in fact the union had been
+	 * removed in full. A reservation list that lies about what it protected
+	 * is worse than no check at all.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0, j = 1; j < ffn_reserve_count; j++) {
+		u64 iend = ffn_reserve[i].base + ffn_reserve[i].size;
+
+		if (ffn_reserve[j].base <= iend) {
+			u64 jend = ffn_reserve[j].base + ffn_reserve[j].size;
+
+			if (jend > iend)
+				ffn_reserve[i].size = jend - ffn_reserve[i].base;
+		} else {
+			i++;
+			ffn_reserve[i] = ffn_reserve[j];
+		}
+	}
+	if (ffn_reserve_count)
+		ffn_reserve_count = i + 1;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ffn_reserve_count; i++)
+		pr_info("FFN: ffn_reserve 0x%llx+0x%llx will be kept out of the kernel's memory map\n",
+			(unsigned long long)ffn_reserve[i].base,
+			(unsigned long long)ffn_reserve[i].size);
+
+	/*
+	 * Say this once, at the end, at error level. A rejected entry never
+	 * reaches the table, so the hit check at the end of plat_mem_setup()
+	 * cannot see it -- without this line the only trace of a lost
+	 * reservation would be a pr_warn scrolled past hundreds of boot
+	 * messages earlier.
+	 */
+	if (rejected)
+		pr_err("FFN: ffn_reserve: %d malformed entry/entries REJECTED -- the regions they named are NOT protected\n",
+		       rejected);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Exclude an arbitrary physical range from the regions obtained in
+ * plat_mem_setup, splitting the chunk when the range falls inside it. Same
+ * shape as memory_exclude_page() above but for a range, and it records how much
+ * it actually removed so that a base which is not in DRAM at all gets reported
+ * rather than silently doing nothing.
+ *
+ * Callers must apply ranges in ascending address order.
+ */
+static __init void memory_exclude_range(struct ffn_reserve_range *r,
+					u64 *mem, u64 *size)
+{
+	u64 rend = r->base + r->size;
+	u64 mend = *mem + *size;
+	u64 drop;
+
+	if (!r->size || !*size)
+		return;
+	if (rend <= *mem || r->base >= mend)
+		return;			/* none of this range is in this chunk */
+
+	/* the part of the chunk below the range is ordinary RAM */
+	if (r->base > *mem) {
+		u64 head = r->base - *mem;
+
+		add_memory_region(*mem, head, BOOT_MEM_RAM);
+		*mem += head;
+		*size -= head;
+	}
+
+	/* drop what the range covers; the caller adds whatever is left */
+	drop = (rend >= mend) ? *size : rend - *mem;
+	*mem += drop;
+	*size -= drop;
+	r->hit += drop;
+}
+
+/*
+ * FFN: ffn_mem=auto -- size Linux's memory from what the board actually has.
+ *
+ * plat_mem_setup() already computes exactly the right number and then throws it
+ * away. cvmx_bootmem_available_mem(mem_alloc_size) sums the cvmx free blocks
+ * that are at least one allocation unit large, which is precisely what the
+ * allocation loop below is able to consume -- blocks smaller than
+ * mem_alloc_size cannot satisfy a request and are skipped, which is why the
+ * memory map has the holes it has. That figure is then clamped to max_memory,
+ * whose compile-time default is 512 MB, so an 8 GiB CP and a 32 GiB DP both
+ * come up with ~442 MB unless somebody passes a per-board mem= constant.
+ *
+ * Those constants are the problem this removes. mem=8G / mem=30G have to be
+ * maintained per board, and they travel on a command line executed by u-boot,
+ * where a stray ';' truncates the line and silently drops everything after it
+ * -- including mem=, reverting the board to 442 MB with no diagnostic at all.
+ * ffn_mem=auto is one token that is correct on every board.
+ *
+ * It deliberately does NOT take everything. What Linux claims comes out of the
+ * cvmx free list, and cvmx still needs that list afterwards: the FPA packet
+ * buffers and WQE pools are allocated from it when the dataplane starts, and
+ * starving them yields "cvmx_fpa3_pool_populate: out of memory" and a dead
+ * packet engine -- which presents exactly like a misprogrammed ring and is not
+ * one.
+ *
+ * So a reserve is held back, derived from the pool sizing the operator has
+ * already declared on the same command line (pktbuf=<count>,<size> and
+ * wqe=<count>,<size>), doubled to cover allocator overhead and the PKO tables,
+ * plus a flat margin. A board running no packet engine declares small pools and
+ * gets a small reserve; a 40-core DP declares large ones and gets a large one.
+ * That is the same derive-rather-than-hardcode rule the reservation list
+ * follows, applied to the last number that was still a constant.
+ *
+ *   ffn_mem=auto            detect, and derive the reserve
+ *   ffn_mem=auto,<size>     detect, but hold back exactly <size>
+ *
+ * The derived reserve is a defensible starting point, not a measured total --
+ * cvmx's full later footprint is not enumerable from here. The boot log states
+ * the figure and how to raise it, so tuning it is an informed act rather than a
+ * guess.
+ *
+ * An explicit mem= always wins. That is detected by checking whether
+ * max_memory still holds its compile-time default, because prom_init() consumes
+ * mem= and does not re-append it to arcs_cmdline -- so unlike every other
+ * argument it cannot be found there afterwards.
+ */
+#define FFN_MEM_MARGIN	(256ull << 20)
+#define FFN_MEM_FLOOR	(256ull << 20)
+
+/* pktbuf=<count>,<size> / wqe=<count>,<size> -> count * size, or 0 */
+static __init u64 ffn_pool_bytes(const char *key)
+{
+	const char *arg = strstr(arcs_cmdline, key);
+	u64 count, size;
+	char *endp;
+
+	if (!arg)
+		return 0;
+
+	arg += strlen(key);
+	count = memparse(arg, &endp);
+	if (endp == arg || *endp != ',')
+		return 0;
+
+	arg = endp + 1;
+	size = memparse(arg, &endp);
+	if (endp == arg)
+		return 0;
+
+	return count * size;
+}
+
+static __init void ffn_mem_auto(u64 avail)
+{
+	const char *arg = strstr(arcs_cmdline, "ffn_mem=auto");
+	u64 reserve = 0, pktbuf, wqe;
+	char *endp;
+
+	if (!arg)
+		return;
+
+	if (max_memory != (512ull << 20)) {
+		pr_info("FFN: ffn_mem=auto ignored -- max_memory is already 0x%llx (mem= or simulation)\n",
+			(unsigned long long)max_memory);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	arg += sizeof("ffn_mem=auto") - 1;
+	if (*arg == ',') {
+		reserve = memparse(arg + 1, &endp);
+		if (endp == arg + 1 || !reserve) {
+			pr_warn("FFN: ffn_mem=auto: unusable reserve, deriving one instead\n");
+			reserve = 0;
+		}
+	}
+
+	pktbuf = ffn_pool_bytes("pktbuf=");
+	wqe = ffn_pool_bytes("wqe=");
+
+	if (!reserve)
+		reserve = 2 * (pktbuf + wqe) + FFN_MEM_MARGIN;
+
+	if (avail <= reserve + FFN_MEM_FLOOR) {
+		pr_warn("FFN: ffn_mem=auto: %llu MB available but the cvmx reserve is %llu MB -- keeping max_memory at %llu MB\n",
+			(unsigned long long)(avail >> 20),
+			(unsigned long long)(reserve >> 20),
+			(unsigned long long)(max_memory >> 20));
+		return;
+	}
+
+	max_memory = avail - reserve;
+
+	pr_info("FFN: ffn_mem=auto: %llu MB detected, holding %llu MB for cvmx (pktbuf %llu MB + wqe %llu MB), Linux takes %llu MB\n",
+		(unsigned long long)(avail >> 20),
+		(unsigned long long)(reserve >> 20),
+		(unsigned long long)(pktbuf >> 20),
+		(unsigned long long)(wqe >> 20),
+		(unsigned long long)(max_memory >> 20));
+	pr_info("FFN: if the FPA pools later fail to populate, raise the reserve with ffn_mem=auto,<size>\n");
+}
+
 void __init plat_mem_setup(void)
 {
 	u64 mem_alloc_size = 4 << 20;
@@ -1121,6 +1471,7 @@
 	u64 total = 0;
 	s64 memory;
 	u64 limit_max, limit_min;
+	int ri;
 	const struct cvmx_bootmem_named_block_desc *named_block;
 	u64 system_limit = cvmx_bootmem_available_mem(mem_alloc_size);
 
@@ -1133,6 +1484,17 @@
 		panic("Must build kernel with CONFIG_NUMA for multi-node system.");
 #endif
 
+	/* must run before anything is handed to add_memory_region() */
+	ffn_reserve_parse();
+
+	/*
+	 * ffn_mem=auto: raise max_memory to what cvmx actually has, less a
+	 * reserve. system_limit already holds the available figure, computed
+	 * with this same mem_alloc_size, and the clamps below then use the new
+	 * max_memory.
+	 */
+	ffn_mem_auto(system_limit);
+
 #ifdef CONFIG_BLK_DEV_INITRD
 	if (rd_name[0]) {
 		const struct cvmx_bootmem_named_block_desc *initrd_block;
@@ -1218,6 +1580,18 @@
 
 		if (memory >= 0) {
 			u64 size = mem_alloc_size;
+
+			/*
+			 * FFN: keep the MP-owned transport ranges out of the
+			 * map. Done before the PCIe-hole pages below so that
+			 * the exclusions stay in ascending address order --
+			 * ffn_reserve ranges sit well below
+			 * CVMX_PCIE_BAR1_PHYS_BASE (0xf0000000), and both
+			 * helpers only move the cursor forward.
+			 */
+			for (ri = 0; ri < ffn_reserve_count; ri++)
+				memory_exclude_range(&ffn_reserve[ri],
+						     (u64 *)&memory, &size);
 			/*
 			 * exclude a page at the beginning and end of
 			 * the 256MB PCIe 'hole' so the kernel will not
@@ -1247,6 +1621,20 @@
 	}
 	cvmx_bootmem_unlock();
 
+	/*
+	 * A base that is not in DRAM, or one already consumed before we got
+	 * here, would otherwise be silently ignored -- and the operator would
+	 * believe a region was protected when it was not.
+	 */
+	for (ri = 0; ri < ffn_reserve_count; ri++) {
+		if (ffn_reserve[ri].hit == ffn_reserve[ri].size)
+			continue;
+		pr_warn("FFN: ffn_reserve 0x%llx+0x%llx: only 0x%llx bytes were excluded -- the remainder is NOT protected, check the base\n",
+			(unsigned long long)ffn_reserve[ri].base,
+			(unsigned long long)ffn_reserve[ri].size,
+			(unsigned long long)ffn_reserve[ri].hit);
+	}
+
 mem_alloc_done:
 
 	/*
@@ -1411,6 +1799,65 @@
 extern const char __appended_dtb;
 extern const char __dtb_octeon_3xxx_begin;
 extern const char __dtb_octeon_68xx_begin;
+
+/*
+ * FFN: locate the device tree by NAME rather than by a hardcoded address.
+ *
+ * u-boot publishes the tree it built for this board as the cvmx_bootmem named
+ * block "__fdt" (on a PA-5220 CP: base 0x80000, size 0x20000 -- read live from
+ * the named-block array at the descriptor in /proc/octeon_info). Looking a
+ * region up by name is what the vendor's own CP software does for everything it
+ * needs, and it is the difference between a constant that happens to be correct
+ * on this board and a kernel that finds its own tree on any reclaimed board --
+ * which is the entire point of using each board's own firmware in place instead
+ * of packaging it.
+ *
+ * ffn_fdt= is kept as an explicit override, and still wins, for the case where
+ * the operator knows something the descriptor does not.
+ */
+#define FFN_FDT_BLOCK "__fdt"
+
+static __init u64 ffn_fdt_from_named_block(void)
+{
+	const struct cvmx_bootmem_named_block_desc *nb;
+	u64 dram_end = (u64)octeon_bootinfo->dram_size << 20;
+	const void *virt;
+
+	nb = cvmx_bootmem_find_named_block(FFN_FDT_BLOCK);
+	if (!nb)
+		return 0;
+
+	if (!nb->base_addr || nb->base_addr >= dram_end) {
